<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vincent of Saragossa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 304</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a> · Also known as Saint Vincent the Deacon</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vincent of Saragossa was a deacon of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a> who was martyred at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a> in the persecution of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a>, around 304. He became one of the most widely venerated saints of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. The Frankish king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/childebert-i">Childebert I</a> brought back relics of him from Spain and founded an abbey in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> to house them, later called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-germain-des-pres">Saint-Germain-des-Prés</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vincent-of-saragossa.jpg" alt="Vincent of Saragossa" width="320" height="516" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tomás Giner · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vicente_de_Zaragoza_by_Tom%C3%A1s_Giner,_1462%E2%80%931466.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Vincent is also known as Vincent Martyr, Vincent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huesca">Huesca</a> or Vincent the Deacon. He was born at Huesca, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a>, in the latter part of the 3rd century; it is believed that his father was Eutricius and his mother Enola, a native of Osca. He spent most of his life in Zaragoza, where he was educated and ordained to the diaconate by Bishop Valerius, who sent him to preach throughout the diocese. Because Valerius suffered from a speech impediment, Vincent acted as his spokesman.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>When the Roman Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a> began persecuting Christians in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, Vincent and Valerius were brought before the governor, Dacian, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a> and imprisoned. The earliest account of Vincent's martyrdom is a poem by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prudentius">Prudentius</a> in his Peristephanon. Offered release if he would consign Scripture to the fire, Vincent refused. He was stretched on the rack, his flesh torn with iron hooks, and burned on a red-hot gridiron, and he died in prison on a floor strewn with broken pottery. The gridiron story is perhaps adapted from the martyrdom of Lawrence, another son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huesca">Huesca</a>. Valerius was exiled.</p><p>According to legend, ravens protected his body from vultures until his followers could recover it, and his remains were taken to what is now Cape St. Vincent. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afonso-i-of-portugal">Afonso I of Portugal</a> had them exhumed in 1173 and brought by ship to Lisbon Cathedral; the transfer appears on the coat of arms of Lisbon. Three elaborated hagiographies, all based on a lost 5th-century Passion, circulated in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, and their Acts have been described as rather freely coloured by the imagination of their compiler.</p><p>His tomb in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a> became the earliest centre of his cult, and veneration spread from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oviedo">Oviedo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beziers">Béziers</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narbonne">Narbonne</a> and Castres, where relics arrived in 863. A church built for him in Visigothic Iberia was razed in 711, and its materials went into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosque-cathedral-of-cordoba">Great Mosque of Córdoba</a>. His feast day is 22 January. He is patron of Valencia, Zaragoza and Portugal, and is shown as a deacon in a dalmatic. The island of São Vicente in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-verde">Cape Verde</a> and Saint Vincent in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> were named for him because they were first sighted on his feast day.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Vincent of Saragossa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>According to traditional accounts, a Visigothic church dedicated to Saint <b>Vincent of Saragossa</b> stood on the site and was shared by Christians and Muslims until 785, when Abd al-Rahman I bought the Christian half for a hundred thousand dinars and demolished it.</blockquote><a href="/work/mosque-cathedral-of-cordoba">Mosque–Cathedral of Córdoba · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Childebert ruled for nearly fifty years, campaigning against the Kingdom of the Burgundians and the Visigoths in Spain, from where he brought back relics of <b>Saint Vincent</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-paris">Kingdom of Paris</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Osca, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in Valentia Edetanorum, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=42.14,-0.41&amp;year=294" aria-label="Osca on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Vincent of Saragossa"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4974 3970h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4974 3970h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=42.14,-0.41&amp;year=294">What was here in 294 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
